\'93Are you high, Tony? What the tax are you on?\'94\
\'93No, nothing. It\'92s just like Little Clinton is trying to show me something\'85 like, just when I put my hand on your belly.\'94\
\'93Then don\'92t do that.\'94\
Chanta took a deep breath and recovered. He stood took her hand and led her toward the master suite, past walls adorned with priceless art and custom displays.\
Something shifted in Sarah\'92s stomach, and it wanted Little Clinton, or the queasiness of pregnancy, but something deeper\'97a revulsion that seemed to emanate from the very air.\
She stopped.\
\'93Sarah?\'94 Chanta noticed her hesitation at the threshold of his bedroom.\
She swallowed and looked up\'97\
A portrait hung just inside. Old oil. Heavy frame. The kind of face that had been taught, from birth, that consequence was something other people experienced. Sarah knew it instantly. The eyes, the mouth, the particular smugness of a man who\'92d built an empire out of other people\'92s lives and called it a legacy.\
Her skin crawled.\
\'93I...\'94 She pressed her palm against the doorframe. \'93You have no idea what that man did to me.\'94\
\'93What man? Shakti? If this is about the wedding\'97\'94\
\'93Jamison Hardy!\'94 The name burst from her lips like a confession. \'93That monster, he... I can\'92t...\'94 She swayed slightly, gripping the wall for support.\
\'93Sarah, Hardy died like a hundred years ago. What are you talking about?\'94\
Horror dawned in her eyes as she realised what she\'92d said. She tried to recover, but her voice shook anyway. \'93I live in his shadow every day, and now so do you.\'94\
Chanta reached for her, concerned. \'93Sarah, you\'92re not making sense. Come here, let me\'97\'94\
But now his touch felt wrong. Not because he was Tony. Because he wasn\'92t, not entirely, anymore. Because everything around him\'97this place, this money, even Tony\'92s clumsy overtures and genuine tenderness\'97felt like it had teeth.\
Sarah abruptly buttoned her blouse with shaking fingers. \'93This was a mistake. I\'92m feeling ill. I need to leave.\'94\
\'93Sarah, wait. What just happened?\'94\
\'93I can\'92t explain it,\'94 she said, backing away from him. \'93I just... I can\'92t be here. I\'92m sorry.\'94\
Chanta\'92s expression darkened with hurt, then defensive anger. \'93Fine. You know what? Go back to your husband, your perfect little family. I\'92ll just call up a dozen Elvines to spend the night with me.\'94\
\'93You\'92ll what?\'94 Sarah\'92s voice went sharp with alarm.\
\'93You heard me,\'94 Chanta said, grabbing his communication device. \'93I\'92ve got numbers for days.\'94\
\'93You\'92ll sleep with them?\'94 Her tone was somewhere between question and accusation, and something else she didn\'92t have a name for.\
Chanta chuckled, misunderstanding her concern. \'93Well, not all of them, of course. I\'92m not that virile.\'94 He paused, smirking.\
Sarah almost reconsidered sleeping with him, just to stop him sleeping around, but one look at Tony convinced her he\'92d always be a rake no matter what she did tonight. \'93You don\'92t understand what you\'92re\'97\'94\
\'93What? Making people happy? Giving them the time of their lives?\'94\
With a choked sob, Sarah fled his penthouse, leaving Chanta standing alone in his perfectly curated paradise, staring at the water glasses and the untouched courses and the view projection.\
He stared at the door for a long moment, as if it might reopen out of sheer appreciation.\
Then he picked up his phone again, still frowning, still wounded, still absolutely certain that the problem was timing, or mood lighting, or the bidet playlist.
